A1. These people are very uppish. ~ SNOBS
A6. Don't depend on these without padlocks. ~ HASPS
A10. Measure of weight. ~ TON
A11. What a fancy stepper does in Argentine. ~ TANGO
A14. This frequently covers a void. ~ CAP
A15. 'Tis said, who does this will not fall. ~ UNITES
A17. X marks this spot. ~ LOCALE
A19. Be truthful under this. ~ OATH
A20. A precious stone. ~ OPAL
A21. This puts a fighter on his feet. ~ GONG
A23. A period of time. ~ ERA
A24. It's hard for a person to be in this. ~ NEED
A26. Not all of these are beasts of burden. ~ ASSES
A28. Something you can both eat—and drink. ~ TOAST
A30. An after thought (abbr.). ~ PS
A31. Half a laugh. ~ HA
A32. To suggest without saying. ~ IMPLY
A36. Many a feul is caught in this. ~ GRATE
A39. European ruler (extinct). ~ TSAR
A40. This catches the dirt. ~ EAR
A41. This is very tiresome. ~ BORE
A43. Not much of anything. ~ IOTA
A45. What a social climber looks for in the Society Column. ~ ITEM
A47. This helps the farmers' hayday. (Tillers of the soil get this). ~ TEDDER
A49. This has a sting in it. ~ NETTLE
A51. This gets drunk in London. ~ ALE
A52. Not ahead and not back. ~ ASIDE
A54. You could catch a crab with this. ~ OAR
A55. On what many a tourist has relieved a long felt thirst. ~ LINER
A56. The Goddess of Tin Pan Alley. ~ MAMMY

D1. What can you get at an apiary? ~ STUNG
D2. A negative prefix. ~ NON
D3. These are not conducive to love's young dream. ~ ONIONS
D4. A sign of agreement the proofreaders will know. ~ STET
D5. Indefinite article. ~ AN
D6. This is easily rolled. ~ HOOP
D7. Something any poor fish has. ~ SCALES
D8. Always a friend. ~ PAL
D9. Do this and you'll make a hit with the girls. ~ SPEND
D12. These follow the fires. ~ ASHES
D13. What villains do when successful. ~ GLOAT
D16. These are always giving away the cost of things. ~ TAGS
D18. The place where Christ performed his first miracle. ~ CANA
D22. A filling station for the Ship of the Desert. ~ OASIS
D25. Lack of this would reduce operating pleasures. ~ ETHER
D27. This would be very hard to hold. ~ EEL
D29. This will get you somewhere if you use pull. ~ OAR
D33. This is often on the mind of man. ~ MAIDEN
D34. This gives one quite a start. ~ PROD
D35. When these go by they are gone forever. ~ YEARS
D36. It takes a lot of this to make a meal. ~ GRIND
D37. This means to countenance or assist. ~ ABET
D38. When a cannibal sees this he wants to beat it. ~ TOMTOM
D39. You should figure well to get this. ~ TOTAL
D42. This polishes things off well. ~ EMERY
D44. What Vertical 3 is apt to cause. ~ TEAR
D46. To be full—not what you are thinking. ~ TEEM
D48. A Yale fellow. ~ ELI
D50. This is a hit. ~ LAM
D53. A conjunction used often by failures. ~ IF


